        "committer": {
          "name": "Artem Blagodarenko",
          "email": "ablagodarenko@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-04 21:01:44.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-20730 llapi: read a namespace walk as LFU chunks",
        "message": "LU-20730 llapi: read a namespace walk as LFU chunks\n\nA client walking a directory with llapi_scan_namespace() also gets one\ncallback per object, and there is no way to read that walk as an LFU\nObject Stream.\n\nAdd LFU_SOURCE_NAMESPACE to llapi_lfu_open(), and let LFU_SOURCE_ANY\nwalk a directory. The walk cannot be paused, so it runs in a thread of\nits own: its callback waits for a read and writes each record into\nthat read's buffer; when the buffer is full it ends the read and holds\nthe record for the next one. The walk's threads take turns at the\nbuffer, and closing the stream cancels the walk.\n\nA walk has no position to resume from, so its cursor counts the\nobjects sent and carries a per-stream token: only the last read's\ncursor goes on, anything else is -ESTALE, and a walk has one shard.\n\nRecords carry the path below the start point in LfuEa.path, the statx\nattributes, and the layout, directory stripe, HSM state and lazy size\nrebuilt in the form the MDT stores, so a consumer decodes them as it\ndoes a device stream's. lfsp_filter is given llp_data.\n\nllapi_scan_test test11, run by sanity 56Ee, walks a tree of 306\nobjects in 2-slot reads of 4 KiB with one and four walk threads, and\nchecks that the stream sends what the callback delivers, with the same\npaths, that a filter drops what it rejects, and that stale cursors, a\nsecond shard and closing mid-walk are handled.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nSigned-off-by: Artem Blagodarenko <ablagodarenko@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm\nChange-Id: If83ef1fbdfe6f5b6c078e3815cceff56e1c41719\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
